-- Q1233: Capital Structure Tax Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 163(j)(7)(B) and Reg. Section 1.168(i)-4, a real property trade or business that elects out of the Section 163(j) limitation must use the Alternative Depreciation System (ADS). For nonresidential real property, ADS requires a 40-year recovery period using the straight-line method. This trade-off allows unlimited interest deductions but extends depreciation schedules.
Wrong (A): MACRS uses a 39-year period for nonresidential real property, but the declining balance method is not used for real property under either system. Moreover, electing out requires ADS, not MACRS.
Wrong (C): The 15-year MACRS recovery period applies to qualified improvement property, not nonresidential real property.
Wrong (D): Depreciation is still allowed after the election. The consequence is switching to ADS with longer recovery periods, not the elimination of depreciation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1233;

-- Q1235: Capital Structure Tax Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 163(j)(4)(B)(ii), disallowed business interest expense from a partnership is allocated to each partner based on their distributive share and carried forward at the partner level, not the entity level. Partner A carries forward 40% of $200,000 = $80,000, treated as business interest expense in succeeding years subject to the partner''s own 163(j) limitation.
Wrong (A): Partnerships do not carry forward disallowed interest at the entity level. Section 163(j)(4) requires allocation to partners for partner-level carryforward.
Wrong (B): The partner-level carryforward remains subject to Section 163(j), not Section 163(d). It retains its character as business interest expense and cannot be deducted against investment income.
Wrong (C): Disallowed business interest is not permanently lost. It carries forward indefinitely at the partner level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1235;

